nda  but nonetheless there's enough evidence  to suggest there in the  for example the reactions of the catin a  discovery in april 1943 demonstrate all  at once there's a little  flash of light on on popular attitudes  towards  uh the treatment of the jews and people  are saying well  um okay the the russians have murdered  these people encountering but look what  we've done to the jews so  these sorts of ideas  these sort of expressions give a little  glimmer of light  into the fact that knowledge of this was  far more widespread  than um than often people  later on were prepared to admit to but  as i said i think we have to  be cautious a little bit in saying what  was this knowledge it was it a  general fading of terrible things  happening sometimes with  some hard evidence about mass shootings  and so on  or was it by knowledge do we mean  a complete understanding of what we  subsequently know happened  the the total nature of the  this colossal genocidal attempt by the  nazis  so i think if you're looking for the  latter then  much of the comment is is honest that we  didn't know  of the scale of this and so on and also  that people didn't know many of the  details but of course  it is the case that the knowledge of  what was going on even the direst  aspects  was more widespread than subsequently  people were prepared to admit to  thank you so much for being here and  thank you for your not only monumental  work but absolutely great read  as peter has said thank you very much  for being here  you
 

